"The even-order M\"obius ladder local antimagic chromatic number conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/339235/the-even-order-m-o-bius-ladder-local-antimagic-chromatic-num)\nciteturn10search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The even-order M{\"o}bius ladder local antimagic chromatic number conjecture\n\nThe even-order M{\"o}bius ladder local antimagic chromatic number conjecture\n\nFor $n\\ge 2$n≥2, let $M_{2n}$M 2 n​ be the M{\"o}bius ladder obtained from the cycle $C_{2n}=u_1u_2\\cdots u_nv_1v_2\\cdots v_nu_1$C 2 n​=u 1​u 2​⋯u n​v 1​v 2​⋯v n​u 1​ by adding the edges $u_iv_i$u i​v i​ for $1\\le i\\le n$1≤i≤n. Let $\\chi_{la}$χ l a​ denote the local antimagic chromatic number.\n\nM{\"o}bius ladder conjecture. For even $n\\ge 4$n≥4,\n\n$$\\chi_{la}(M_{2n})=4.$$χ l a​(M 2 n​)=4.\n\nThe surrounding results establish the value for odd $n\\ge 3$n≥3 and note that $M_4=K_4$M 4​=K 4​ has local antimagic chromatic number $4$4. The even-order case stated here is presented as a conjecture, with no resolution supplied in the text.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nGee-Choon Lau, Wai-Chee Shiu and Ho-Kuen Ng, “On local antimagic chromatic number of cycle-related join graphs”, arXiv:1805.04888 (2018).\n\ngraph-coloringlocal-antimagic-labelingmobius-ladders\n\n0\n\n1 week ago\n\n## Solutions 0\n\nSign in to submit a solution.\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the conjectures of neighbor locating coloring of graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304397522002559)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # On the conjectures of neighbor locating coloring of graphs ... We show that, for each pair of integers with , there exists a connected graph G such that and , which proves the first conjecture.\n\n## Theoretical Computer Science\n\nVolume 922, 24 June 2022, Pages 300-307\n\n# On the conjectures of neighbor locating coloring of graphs\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2022.04.031Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet be a simple graph. Any partition of to k independent subsets is called a k-coloring of G. If is a minimum such partition, then k is a chromatic number of G, denoted by . A k-coloring is said to be a (metric-)locating coloring, (an ML-coloring), if for every pair of distinct vertices , with same color, there exists a color class {S}_{j} such that . Minimum k for ML-coloring of a graph G, is called (metric-)locating chromatic number of G. A k-neighbor locating coloring of G is a partition of to such that for two vertices , there is a color class {S}_{j} for which, one of them has a neighbor in {S}_{j} and the other not. The minimum k with this property, is said to be neighbor-locating chromatic number of G, denoted by of G. We initiate to continue the study of neighbor locating coloring of graphs which has been already introduced by other authors. In [1] the authors posed three conjectures and we study these conjectures. We show that, for each pair of integers with , there exists a connected graph G such that and , which proves the first conjecture. If G and H are connected graphs, then , that disproves the second conjecture. Finally, we investigate for a family of graphs G, , where is the Mycielski graph of G, that proves the third conjecture for some families of graphs.\n\n## Keywords\n\nColoring\n\nNeighbor locating\n\nConjectures\n\nNeighbor locating coloring\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNEIGHBOR LOCATING COLORING ON THE PRODUCT OF GRAPHS (https://gcgt.ug.edu.pl/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Mojdeh.pdf)\nciteturn10search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2